{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 1, :comments ["<b>And he went not, as at other times, to seek enchantments</b> — for in the former times he practiced divination and wished to curse them by enchantment, and HaShem came upon him by chance, not with his intention for prophecy nor by virtue that he had attained. But now, when it was said to him, \"For there is no enchantment in Jacob, nor divination in Israel\" — neither to harm them nor to benefit them — he abandoned the enchantments and did not go as at other times to meet them. Instead he set his face toward the wilderness, where Israel was, so that he would see them and prepare himself for the divine speech to rest upon him from HaShem, as had happened with him twice, and so it was for him. Therefore it says, \"And the spirit of Elohim came upon him,\" for now the hand of HaShem was upon him as it is upon the prophets, as it says: \"Would that all the people of HaShem were prophets, when HaShem puts His spirit upon them\" (Numbers 11:29), and it says: \"The spirit of HaShem Elohim is upon me\" (Isaiah 61:1). And therefore he now called himself \"hearing the words of El\" (Numbers 24:4), for he is a prophet.\n\nAnd Rashi wrote (Rashi on Numbers 24:1): \"\"And he set his face toward the wilderness\" — as its translation, for the Rav translated it: \"and he set his face toward the wilderness wherein the children of Israel made the calf.\" But in precise manuscripts of the translation of Onkelos (Onkelos on Numbers 24:1) it is not so; rather, it is written in some of them that was corrected from the Jerusalem Targum, and the primary interpretation is what we have explained.\n\nAnd it says: \"who sees the vision of Shaddai\" (ibid.), for he now saw in an illuminating speculum like the former prophets, concerning whom it says: \"And I appeared to Abraham, to Isaac, and to Jacob as El Shaddai\" (Exodus 6:3), or at a level below them, for \"the vision of Shaddai\" is not Shaddai Himself. Behold, they saw in El Shaddai, but he sees in the vision of Shaddai — and these are two levels below them. And therefore he called himself \"with eyes unveiled,\" which is the level of the sons of the prophets, as it says: \"HaShem, open his eyes,\" etc. (II Kings 6:20). And it was already stated likewise concerning Balaam himself at the vision of the angel: \"And HaShem uncovered the eyes of Balaam\" (Numbers 22:31).\n\nDo not think otherwise than what we have explained on account of the statement of our Sages in the Sifrei (Sifrei, Berakhah 39): \"\"And there arose not a prophet since in Israel like Moses\" — in Israel there did not arise, but among the nations of the world one did arise. And who was he? Balaam.\" Rather, there is a distinction between the prophecy of Moses and the prophecy of Balaam: Moses did not know what He was speaking with him, but Balaam knew what He was speaking with him, as it says, \"the utterance of him who hears the words of El.\" Moses did not know when He would speak with him, as it says, \"who speaks with him\" (Numbers 7:89), but Balaam knew when He would speak with him, as it says, \"and knowing the knowledge of the Most High.\" Moses would speak with Him standing, as it says, \"But as for you, stand here with Me\" (Deuteronomy 5:28), but with Balaam He would speak while he was falling, as it says, \"falling and with eyes unveiled.\" A parable: to a king's butcher, who knows how many expenditures go out for the king's table.\n\nThe explanation of the matter of this baraita: since Scripture says, \"And there arose not a prophet since in Israel like Moses, whom HaShem knew face to face\" (Deuteronomy 34:10), and our Sages interpreted that it does not come to recount the superiority of the prophecy of Moses over the prophecy of the rest of the prophets, for He had already informed us of his superiority over them in two places: in the verse \"And I appeared to Abraham, to Isaac, and to Jacob as El Shaddai, but by My name YHWH I did not make Myself known to them\" (Exodus 6:3), and in the verse \"If there be a prophet among you,\" etc. (Numbers 12:6). But now He did not speak of Moses knowing HaShem, in the sense of what is said, \"Make known to me Your ways that I may know You\" (Exodus 33:13), for Scripture did not say \"who knew HaShem face to face\" but said \"whom He knew.\" Rather, it speaks of the elucidation of prophecy. It says that to Moses our teacher came prophecy elucidated, like one speaking to his friend face to face, informing him of His words and intention, so that it would be recognized in his face that he understood His words and intention in the speaking and in the recognition of His will in his face. And they said that this was so for Balaam at the time he prophesied for Israel's glory, for he understood the words of HaShem and His will and desire in the entire future matter to come upon Israel.\n\nEven so, there is a distinction between the prophecy of Moses and Balaam, for Moses did not know what He would speak with him — in what matter, in what commandment He would command him — but he was prepared at all times for the divine speech, and the Holy One, blessed be He, would command him according to what was the will before Him. But Balaam would direct his thought and calculate concerning that matter which he desired, and go and seclude himself and prepare himself for the spirit to rest upon him — perhaps HaShem would chance upon him, as is explicit here. And he knew that if it came upon him, it would be in that matter which he had pondered that He would speak with him, not in another matter. And Moses did not know when He would speak with him, for there was no fixed time for the divine speech with him, but at any time Moses wished and directed his heart to the divine speech, He would speak with him, as it says: \"Stand, and I will hear what HaShem will command\" (Numbers 9:8). And likewise at any time the will would arise before Him, may He be exalted, to command him, he would hear the voice speaking to him from the Tent of Meeting. But Balaam would designate the hour when the divine speech would come to him, and the spirit would not rest upon him except at that hour — perhaps it is that hour which our Sages mention in tractate Berakhot (Berakhot 7a) and in tractate Sanhedrin (Sanhedrin 105b) in which he would curse, and at that hour the spirit would come upon him, never at another time.\n\nAnd likewise the matter of standing is a superiority for Moses, and falling is a deficiency for Balaam, who could not endure prophecy, as it says: \"If we continue to hear the voice of HaShem our Elohim any more, we shall die,\" etc., \"You draw near, and hear\" (Deuteronomy 5:22–24). And they gave the parable of the king's butcher for the first distinction, and their intention is to say that the butcher knows the expenditures of the king's table, but his minister who is trusted in all his house and stands in his council does not know the table's expenditures. And this parable indicates that the Sages hold that Balaam would know of himself, after his contemplation, that HaShem would say to him, \"How shall I curse? El has not cursed\" (Numbers 23:8), and the whole matter, and afterward he would hear the divine speech in those very words which he had pondered in his heart. And this is what I noted — that he was a diviner and the future events would come into his mind, and now, for Israel's sake, he would also hear the divine speech in them. Therefore he prided himself now, saying, \"the utterance of him who hears the words of El.\" And I saw this matter presented differently in the Sifrei, stated in another style in the aggadah of Bemidbar Sinai Rabbah (Numbers Rabbah 20:1), but I need not elaborate.\n\nThe general principle is that their intention is to say that the prophecy of Balaam came to him in words elucidated to him, for the reason they stated — so that the nations of the world would not have an opening to say, \"Had we had a prophet like Moses, we would have served the Holy One, blessed be He.\" But the level of his prophecy is below that of the rest of the prophets, for it is in the vision of Shaddai, as we have explained. And so they said in Vayikra Rabbah (1:13): the Holy One, blessed be He, does not reveal Himself to the prophets of the nations of the world except in half-speech, as it is written, \"And Elohim happened to meet (ויקר) Balaam,\" but to the prophets of Israel in complete speech, as it says, \"And He called (ויקרא) to Moses.\" And half-speech — you will understand its meaning from what we have explained."], :book :numbers, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 24, :notes {0 "Ramban quotes Rashi as citing 'the Rav' (הרב), meaning Rashi's citing a Targum rendering; here it refers to Rashi's own paraphrastic translation, not to Maimonides. The phrase 'אין לי להאריך' (I need not elaborate) marks Ramban's choice to defer a fuller discussion of the variant in Numbers Rabbah."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T04:08:57.843410236Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}